artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a peer-to-peer (P2P) system consisting of a dynamic collection of autonomous nodes (peers), each storing a local relational dataset described by its own schema. Peers communicate only through an overlay network without any centralized coordinator, index, or global catalog, and the set of online peers as well as the data they hold may change over time. A query language L is said to be SQL-rich if it supports at least selection, projection, join across relations residing on different peers, aggregation (such as SUM, COUNT, AVG, MIN, MAX), grouping, and nested subqueries, with semantics equivalent to standard SQL evaluated over the union of the peers' relations (modulo schema mediation).

Design a distributed search and query-evaluation mechanism M for such a P2P system that:

(a) accepts arbitrary queries q in an SQL-rich language L issued at any peer;
(b) returns answers that are sound and as complete as possible with respect to the data currently available in the network;
(c) achieves "reasonable performance," made precise as the requirement that, for queries q of size |q| over a network of N peers, the expected total message count, per-peer communication, and end-to-end latency each grow sub-linearly in N (for example, polylogarithmically in N) under a specified churn and data-distribution model, while preserving correctness guarantees in (a)–(b).

The problem is: does such a mechanism M exist, and if so, give an explicit architecture (overlay structure, indexing/routing scheme, query-decomposition and execution strategy, and schema-mediation layer) realizing it, together with a proof of its correctness and performance bounds? If no mechanism meeting (a)–(c) simultaneously exists, identify alternative formulations (e.g., relaxed completeness, restricted query fragments of L, or probabilistic guarantees) under which a positive solution can be obtained, and provide such a solution.

